{
  "dom_id": "step-4546",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "Consider the graph of the parabola ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/f218c572691a74963cd3808bf4df2923122e84b65d1fabf1138bab6264b043b5.svg) shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/450251899f728a3cc927b72fdb7655204828c8e0be0432cb08cee2462d6e5bfe.png)\n\nWe can describe many features of the graph using mathematical terminology:\n\n- The ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-intercepts of the parabola are at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/8171bb8403bd612f56ef5f32f400f1aef8efeed51ea279d36779771fdea1ae0d.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/779cec712506e0f798805963ab85841b46650fb22681d97a64aec4b85a47d07f.svg) We say that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/b064281df4bef2506521322c7477468b9cc565616e2c5b6f21000df6522399c1.svg) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/1fb9f2f73a09599931892c1dee7629975b4d851e5dd6f0d07b7c641bbf17bf54.svg) are the **roots** (or **zeros**) of the parabola.\n\n- The parabola intersects the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis at  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/6a0e2cbf9f56c3c989ad96f020fe8bb0f7c785ff6de8073c476232b95476ac13.svg). We say that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) is the **![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept**. The ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept is the same as the constant term of the function.\n\n- The parabola has a \"turning point\" at  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/ef43561f9d825c9e79fb03dffafa744d386d0c2a5a07da903675bca120d59fd1.svg) which is called the **vertex**. In an upwards parabola (like the one shown above), the vertex is the minimum point, while in a downwards parabola, the vertex is the maximum point.\n\n- The vertical line  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/df9822243c11ec9f0fabca5cc578976d0bdea2c521b4f61964f5f3850a970504.svg) that passes through the vertex is called the **axis of symmetry**. The part of the parabola to the left of the axis of symmetry is the mirror image of the right, and vice versa.\n\n- This particular parabola is **upward opening** because it has a distinctive ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/13ab0b72e7963f3e74404b8b75f12654ece94c2c44e1fb09fdf230b976eb681b.svg) shape. A parabola ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is upward opening if the quadratic coefficient is positive ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/d2a0fc4d90b45e06999c89b1a40ff6f01116e9c429c195adbfced573f9e47660.svg)\n\n- Parabolas can also be **downward opening** with a distinctive ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/57872695daf3067460adec5a506cfb1457729de4eeac879eab9f7355570bac11.svg) shape. A parabola ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/7c49eede8f0eb80dadd8384ae942dc8248ef454eeb6afa1aeba7ae48e7545456.svg) is downward opening if the quadratic coefficient is negative ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14217628/assets/a16d199f292dbd74941402b3d485cf107a7a985d28aeb13ab50ff6baf87f2d4b.svg)\n\nLet's take a look at an example of a downward opening parabola.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
  "html": "<div id=\"step-4546\" class=\"step\" style=\"display: block\" stepid=\"4546\" steptype=\"tutorial\" contentid=\"1643\">\n            <div class=\"stepHeader\">\n                <div class=\"stepName\">\n                    <a id=\"stepAnchor-4546\" class=\"stepAnchor\" name=\"step-1\">Introduction</a>\n                </div>\n                <div class=\"helpButton\" stepid=\"4546\">?</div>\n            </div> \n\n            <p>Consider the graph of the parabola <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.464ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"16.124ex\" height=\"2.351ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -833.9 7127 1038.9\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><path data-c=\"1D466\" d=\"M21 287Q21 301 36 335T84 406T158 442Q199 442 224 419T250 355Q248 336 247 334Q247 331 231 288T198 191T182 105Q182 62 196 45T238 27Q261 27 281 38T312 61T339 94Q339 95 344 114T358 173T377 247Q415 397 419 404Q432 431 462 431Q475 431 483 424T494 412T496 403Q496 390 447 193T391 -23Q363 -106 294 -155T156 -205Q111 -205 77 -183T43 -117Q43 -95 50 -80T69 -58T89 -48T106 -45Q150 -45 150 -87Q150 -107 138 -122T115 -142T102 -147L99 -148Q101 -153 118 -160T152 -167H160Q177 -167 186 -165Q219 -156 247 -127T290 -65T313 -9T321 21L315 17Q309 13 296 6T270 -6Q250 -11 231 -11Q185 -11 150 11T104 82Q103 89 103 113Q103 170 138 262T173 379Q173 380 173 381Q173 390 173 393T169 400T158 404H154Q131 404 112 385T82 344T65 302T57 280Q55 278 41 278H27Q21 284 21 287Z\"></path></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(767.8,0)\"><path data-c=\"3D\" d=\"M56 347Q56 360 70 367H707Q722 359 722 347Q722 336 708 328L390 327H72Q56 332 56 347ZM56 153Q56 168 72 173H708Q722 163 722 153Q722 140 707 133H70Q56 140 56 153Z\"></path></g><g data-mml-node=\"msup\" transform=\"translate(1823.6,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><path data-c=\"1D465\" d=\"M52 289